There are $n$ sticks of lengths $1, 2, \ldots, n$. Is it possible to form a square from these sticks, and if not, what is the minimum number of sticks that need to be broken in half to form a square:
a) when $n = 12$;
b) when $n = 15$?

s:
To solve the problem of dividing the figure into 8 identical parts, we can proceed as follows:

1. Identify the shape of the figure and assume it is a cube (as suggested by common geometry problems and the typical folktale context).

2. **Divide using plane cuts:**

   **Method 1:**

   a. **First Cut:**

   Make a horizontal cut (parallel to the base) through the middle of the cube, effectively dividing it into two equal halves. These are now two smaller, identical rectangular prisms.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{Cube} \\
   \text{(Make horizontal cut in the middle)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   b. **Second Cut:**

   Make a vertical cut (perpendicular to the base and parallel to one pair of opposite faces) through the middle of the now-separated halves. This will result in four equal parts.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{2 Halves} \\
   \text{(Make vertical cut in the middle of each half)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   c. **Third Cut:**

   Make another vertical cut (perpendicular to the previous vertical cut and to the base) in the middle of the four resulting parts, yielding a total of 8 identical parts.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{4 Parts} \\
   \text{(Make another vertical cut in the middle of each part)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   Hence, each part is an identical smaller cube of one-eighth the volume of the original figure.

   **Method 2:**

   a. **First Cut:**

   Make a vertical cut (parallel to one pair of opposite faces) through the middle of the cube.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{Cube} \\
   \text{(Make vertical cut in the middle)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   b. **Second Cut:**

   Rotate the cube 90 degrees and make another vertical cut (perpendicular to the first cut) through the middle.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{Two Half Parts} \\
   \text{(Make another vertical cut)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   c. **Third Cut:**

   Make horizontal cuts (parallel to the base) through the middle of each of the resulting parts.

   \[
   \begin{array}{c}
   \text{Four Parts} \\
   \text{(Make horizontal cut in the middle of each part)}
   \end{array}
   \]

   This method also results in 8 identical smaller cubes.

Using either method, we freshly obtain 8 identical smaller cubes that are a fraction of the original cube's volume. 

**Conclusion:**

Both methods involve making strategic plane cuts to achieve the goal of dividing the initial shape into 8 identical parts with confidence that each resulting piece is equivalent in form and dimension.

Therefore, the solution to the problem can be expressed mathematically and diagrammatically as methods illustrated. $\boxed{}

